add('lesson:'+defense,'Container for control visibility/enforcement, denial-of-service mechanisms and controlled secure access paths.',[
 'Profile replaces the authored header; all three sections are source exam-tagged. Broad3.2/4.5 tags and Applied label are metadata, not precise approved scope or calibrated difficulty.',
 'Position, operating mode and configuration bound each control. Neither a vendor/category label, related lesson nor the container approves independent assessments, observed prevention or remediation.',
 'This contextual IDS/IPS teaching does not repair the separately held categorical PBQ wording or any prior source/identity hold.'
]);
add('keywords:'+defense,'Brief terms distinguish traffic-rule controls, policy-capable zones and secure-context VPN connectivity.',[
 'A firewall can apply configured traffic policy; it is not proof every application action or encrypted payload is understood. Segmentation boundaries require actual allowed-flow policy, not merely a zone name.',
 'VPN is introductory protected-path vocabulary in this secure lesson context; protection depends on configuration, trust and keys. Not every service called VPN encrypts every flow or secures compromised endpoints.'
]);
add('check:'+defense,'Reveal-only example explains why expensive application requests can exhaust service resources without filling link bandwidth.',[
 'The stated expensive-request/overload premise supports application-layer resource exhaustion, not confirmed malicious intent, a botnet or automatic DDoS attribution.',
 'Request-aware controls may suit the bottleneck; authentication, WAF, caching, scrubbing or scaling is not universally sufficient. This source tests no service/traffic or mitigated outcome.',
 'Model response is revealed without learner-response input, grading key, automatic correctness or wrong-response feedback; no new question or independent family.'
]);
add('section:'+defense+':0','Six control comparisons distinguish state context, observed detection, configured inline blocking, application awareness and consolidated/web filtering.',[
 'Inline network-IPS example is the source context, not a claim that every host/prevention product uses the same network topology. Can block depends on visibility/mode/policy; out-of-band detection needs an enforcement path to stop traffic.',
 'Stateful policy is not unrestricted established-session permission. NGFW/UTM/WAF capabilities vary; encryption, licensing, configuration, false positives, capacity and failure concentration matter.',
 'Filtering HTTP requests does not replace secure coding, endpoint controls or all application authorization. No firewall rules, prevention, decrypted contents or device deployment is verified.'
]);
add('section:'+defense+':1','Differentiate distributed origins from volumetric, protocol/state and application-resource exhaustion mechanisms.',[
 'Many sources describe distribution; an expensive request or traffic volume alone does not establish attacker identity or maliciousness. Organic demand and attack evidence require context.',
 'Rate limits/caching/upstream scrubbing/scaling/application controls target different bottlenecks; no guaranteed stop or unlimited elastic capacity. IDS alerts and inline IPS matches cannot fix an already saturated upstream link alone.'
]);
add('section:'+defense+':2','Distinguish administrative entry, client/server proxy roles, VPN endpoint topology and selected TLS inspection.',[
 'Jump hosts need hardening, authorization and monitored paths; naming a host does not enforce privileged access. Forward/reverse proxy roles describe whom they act for, not full attack coverage.',
 'Secure-context site-to-site versus remote access describes network/endpoint connections under configured trust/key assumptions, not every flow protected or safe endpoints.',
 'TLS interception terminates trust at inspection hops. Certificate/pinning/mutual-authentication and privacy/performance/legal constraints require approved context and supported integration; source exception language is not permission to bypass verification. No traffic, CA, key or legal configuration is changed.'
]);
add('traps:'+defense,'Three source cautions separate detection/enforcement, application denial of service and application CSRF defenses from WAF filtering.',[
 'IDS detection does not itself establish an enforcement path or automatic prevention; DDoS mechanisms extend beyond bandwidth.',
 'The CSRF caution positions application token/cookie design rather than WAF as the defense. SameSite is normally defense in depth under applicable cookie/browser/site assumptions, not a universal replacement for validated CSRF tokens; XSS and same-site cross-origin cases remain relevant.',
 'No application is audited or repaired, and no control label proves malicious requests were blocked.'
]);
add('lesson:'+protocols,'Container for transport properties, bounded registered-port conventions, file-transfer distinctions and common TLS1.3 flow.',[
 'Profile replaces authored header and marks DHCP-conflict section2 optional Beyond the textbook. Broad3.2/4.5 labels are not precise child scope; authored chapter/profile domain3 drives existing coarse chapter review.',
 'Ports are conventions rather than verified application identity or benignness. The common certificate-based TLS1.3 example is not every PSK/resumption/early-data flow or full protocol conformance.'
]);
add('keywords:'+protocols,'Brief TLS, VPN and firewall entries introduce distinct channel-protection and traffic-policy purposes.',[
 'Modern TLS protection requires correct endpoint verification, negotiated protocol/keys and trustworthy endpoints; purpose wording does not guarantee all data paths or configurations are safe.',
 'VPN and firewall meanings repeat the other guide under different pools/order. No new canonical mapping, independent subject family, controlled route or actual outcome is inferred.'
]);
add('check:'+protocols,'Reveal-only UDP443 example rejects identifying web traffic solely from a TCP-versus-UDP assumption.',[
 'HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP, including conventional443 and other permitted UDP ports. Seeing UDP443 does not prove HTTP/3, benign HTTPS or a specific application.',
 'Identify the actual negotiated/application protocol with suitable evidence. The revealed source answer accepts no learner response and provides no key comparison, score, wrong-response feedback or independent assessment.'
]);
add('section:'+protocols+':0','Transport and seven registered-service examples distinguish ordered TCP streams, UDP datagrams and conditional service conventions.',[
 'TCP retransmission/congestion control do not guarantee a connection survives every failure; UDP has no built-in ordering/delivery guarantee but applications can add reliability. No blanket UDP-always-unreliable or safe-port inference.',
 'DNS53 supports TCP and UDP. DHCPv4 server67/client68 is typical initial acquisition; relays/known-state traffic need not all be broadcast. HTTPS443 can include HTTP/3 over QUIC/UDP and nondefault ports.',
 'LDAP389 TCP/UDP and RDP3389 TCP/UDP registrations do not imply identical implementations or full directory sessions over UDP. Protect directory authentication; TCP636 is a common LDAPS convention, not every389 connection encrypted.',
 'SMTP25 relay differs from587 submission; transport security depends on the actual protocol/configuration. POP3S995 convention does not identify arbitrary observed traffic or prove a trusted peer. No packets, registrations outside selected rows or actual services are inspected.'
]);
add('section:'+protocols+':1','Separate FTP-over-TLS from SSH file transfer and describe a bounded certificate-based TLS1.3 authenticated key agreement.',[
 'FTP control/data paths differ from SFTP over SSH; common TCP22 is a convention, not a universal endpoint or approval of the separately held SSH glossary.',
 'The certificate-based example uses negotiated shared keying, server signature proof and Finished transcript checks. Trust/name/chain validation still matters; a signature alone does not confer trust.',
 'TLS1.3 removes static RSA key transport; RSA signatures are a different use. PSK/resumption/0-RTT paths differ, and early data has replay constraints. Source common-flow language is not blanket forward secrecy, every-flow ordering or a full cryptographic implementation.'
]);
add('section:'+protocols+':2','Optional DHCP-conflict context distinguishes managed leases and safeguards from universal address uniqueness.',[
 'Source explicitly rejects DHCP-prevents-all-conflicts. Static/rogue/overlapping-scope conditions can still conflict; leases, exclusions, reservations and detection require suitable configuration.',
 'Optional enrichment is not newly mandatory exam scope, a guaranteed conflict-free network, protection against rogue servers or a tested address assignment.'
]);
add('traps:'+protocols,'Three cautions retain RDP registered transport nuance, FTPS/SFTP separation and modern TLS terminology.',[
 'Registered TCP/UDP3389 is not guaranteed actual transport use or benign RDP traffic. FTPS differs from SFTP even though both can support secure transfer under their own configurations.',
 'Modern TLS versus obsolete SSL wording does not approve every TLS version/cipher or historical protocol setting. No connection, cipher selection or endpoint is verified.'
]);
